#
# Scenario (0): negative control - both priority options are
# omitted, so no "Setting thread ... nice to" line is
# printed at all, and every I/O, purge and master thread keeps
# whatever nice value it inherited from the process (compared
# against the observed nice of the main thread, not a hardcoded
# 0 - MTR itself may not start mysqld at nice 0).
# --innodb-purge-threads=2 forces a purge worker thread to exist:
# the default is 1 purge thread on <=16 CPU hosts, which is only
# the coordinator (srv_start_purge_threads() creates worker
# threads for i=1..purge_threads-1, srv0start.cc).
#
# restart: --innodb-purge-threads=2
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (0), step 0: area after this restart contains a completed start]
include/assert_grep.inc [scenario (0): no thread nice-setting attempt while both options are omitted]
#
# Scenario (1): both sysvars set to distinct positive values (no
# privilege needed to raise nice) - every I/O thread gets the I/O
# nice, every purge thread (coordinator and workers) gets the
# purge nice, and the master and main threads stay at the
# baseline recorded in scenario (0). Distinct values so a bug
# reading the wrong sysvar in either group would be caught; both
# are above that baseline, since raising a nice needs no privilege
# while lowering one does.
#
# restart
#
# Scenario (2): final restart back to default options - required
# for the mutation gate, which needs the next test in the
# queue to be able to reuse this server: while $restart_opts is
# left set (a non-default my.cnf line still applied), MTR always
# restarts the next queued test, and check_testcase() also does
# not run for a server with $restart_opts set. The bare "restart:"
# form (colon, nothing after) clears $restart_opts entirely - see
# mysql-test-run.pl's parsing of the .expect file. Nothing is
# asserted here on purpose.
#
# restart:
